explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nJ: 1, 7: 1, 5: 1\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♣10\nsingle: ♣Q\nsingle: ♠K\nsingle: ♦A\nsingle: ♠2\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': "**My Immediate Thought Process:**\n\nOkay, here's the deal. The situation is pretty tight. My teammate just played a 4, and the landlord is up next with only one card left. I've got a hand with a bunch of high cards, but that pesky 10 is my lowest. The unknown cards are a J, 7, and 5. I need to figure out who has what and how to prevent the landlord from winning immediately.\n\nFirst, let's establish the obvious. My hand has six cards. My teammate played a 4. The landlord has one card. That accounts for all the cards in play. This means the J, 7, and 5 are the only cards left.\n\nNow, here's where it gets interesting. I need to make a move that either:\n\n1.  Blocks the landlord completely, forcing them to pass (if they have a single that is greater than 4), or\n2.  Ensures that *I* go out first so my teammate can follow if Landlord is a bit too clever for us.\n\nMy instinct is to block, but I need to do the math. If the Landlord has the Jack, Seven, or Five, any card will beat the 4 except for the small cards. Now, do I go with the high card for a guaranteed block but leave my 10 as vulnerable?\n\nIf I play a high card, the landlord passes and then my teammate can't play if he has one. I get stuck with my low cards and maybe the Landlord will win after I play it again.\n\nBut if I go with my 10, and it's higher than the Landlord's, great. My team can get the upper hand on the game.\n\nThe key is preventing the Landlord from getting a free win with a low card or being able to play it again with 2 cards.\n\nI can't just pass because the Landlord automatically goes.\n\nI can't put down a high card if I will have nothing left to beat the Landlord with later.\n\nI have no bombs or straights. I'm stuck with the 10, the Qs, and the Ace.\n\nIt seems I have to accept a risk on the 10. The only way to save my teammate is to put up the 10, which they can beat.\n\nI need to put up the 10.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The Teammate led a ♥4. The Landlord (Next) has only 1 card remaining. The cards unknown are J, 7, and 5. This means the Landlord holds one of J, 7, or 5. \n- If the Landlord holds the J (highest remaining card), he will win the game regardless of my play (he beats my 10 now, or waits to beat my 10 later as I must eventually play it).\n- If the Landlord holds the 7 or 5, my ♣10 is sufficient to beat the Landlord (10 > 7 > 5).\n- Playing a higher card like Q, K, A, or 2 also works, but it leaves the ♣10 in my hand as a potential liability to be played later. \n- Passing is not an option because if the Landlord holds the 5 or 7, passing lets him win immediately.\n\nReason: I must block the Landlord. Since the outcome against a Landlord holding a J is a loss anyway, I should play optimally against the scenarios where we can win (Landlord holding 7 or 5). Playing the ♣10 beats the 4 and is high enough to force a pass from a Landlord holding 7 or 5. This move also clears my lowest card, leaving me with a very strong hand (Q, Q, K, A, 2) to secure the win or help the teammate. If the Landlord passes the 10, the Teammate (who would hold the J) can take the lead and finish the game.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♣10"]}'}
